 3.1 Causal Formulation and Real-Time Inference 3.1.1 Streaming inference setup 3.2 Knowledge Distillation 3.2.1 Output-level distillation 3.2.2 Intermediate feature distillation 3.2.3 Progressive ramp-up and training objective 4 Experiments 4.1 Dataset and evaluation protocol 4.2 Quality–latency trade-off of cTF-Mamba 4.3 Knowledge distillation: 8-layer → \\rightarrow 1-layer 4.4 Ablation studies 4.4.1 Hybrid Mamba–Transformer teachers 4.5 Comparison with prior causal and real-time models 5 Conclusion 6 Generative AI Use Disclosure References License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2608.12099v1 [cs.SD] 12 Aug 2026 Chao Huang La Quatra Siniscalchi Cheng Fu Tsao RT-SEMamba: Real-Time Speech Enhancement Mamba via Progressive Knowledge Distillation Rong Sung-Feng Moreno Sabato Marco Wen-Huang Szu-Wei Yu Abstract We present RT-SEMamba, a fully causal speech enhancement (SE) model built upon causal time–frequency Mamba blocks. Unlike Transformer-based architectures that rely on a growing key–value cache, Mamba propagates a fixed-size recurrent state per layer, enabling memory- and bandwidth-efficient long-form inference. We further introduce a progressive knowledge distillation (KD) strategy that compresses an 8-layer teacher into a shallow 1-layer student by jointly distilling complex spectral outputs and intermediate representations. On Voicebank-DEMAND, the 8-layer RT-SEMamba achieves 3.32 PESQ with a 25 ms algorithmic latency constraint, and the distilled 1-layer student improves over a naive 1-layer baseline from 3.06 to 3.18 PESQ while preserving the same steady-state RTF, delivering a 2.75 × \\times speedup over the teacher. These results demonstrate that state-space models with progressive KD provide a competitive quality–latency trade-off for real-time SE. keywords speech enhancement, real-time processing, knowledge distillation, state-space models, Mamba † † address: 1 Academia Sinica, Taiwan 2 National Taiwan University, Taiwan 3 Kore University of Enna, Italy 4 University of Palermo, Italy 5 NVIDIA † † email: roychao@cmlab.csie.ntu.edu.tw, yu.tsao@citi.sinica.edu.tw 1 Introduction Real-time speech enhancement (SE) is a key component in interactive audio applications such as hearing aids [ 45 , 2 ] , cochlear implants [ 22 ] , AR/VR communication [ 49 ] , and live teleconferencing [ 17 ] . In these scenarios, the SE front-end must satisfy tight algorithmic latency and real-time factor (RTF) constraints while robustly handling non-stationary noise and reverberation. Unlike non-causal SE, streaming SE can only rely on past and current observations, yet still needs to deliver artifact-free speech under limited compute and memory resources [ 8 , 41 ] . Modern SE systems are typically formulated as supervised regression from noisy waveforms or time–frequency representations to their clean targets [ 27 , 31 , 44 , 21 ] . Convolutional, recurrent, and hybrid convolutional–recurrent networks [ 11 , 7 , 23 , 39 ] , as well as Transformer-style architectures [ 20 , 1 , 28 ] , achieve strong performance in non-causal benchmarks. Phase-aware, complex-spectrum, sub-band, and metric-oriented models further improve time–frequency enhancement quality [ 50 , 18 , 15 , 10 , 12 ] . More recently, diffusion-based generative models have been explored as an alternative SE formulation [ 29 , 33 , 35 , 47 , 24 , 13 ] . However, many Transformer- and diffusion-based SE systems are evaluated with non-causal future context, making low-latency streaming deployment less straightforward. To better match streaming requirements, several works have proposed explicitly causal and low-latency SE architectures [ 9 , 40 , 36 ] . Waveform-domain models demonstrate real-time operation on commodity hardware [ 9 ] , and causal front-ends built on self-supervised representations show that semantic priors can partially compensate for limited context [ 40 ] . More recently, Mamba-style selective state-space models [ 14 ] have been introduced as linear-complexity alternatives to self-attention, and have been adapted to time–frequency speech enhancement and separation [ 4 , 25 , 46 , 3 , 19 , 5 ] . By propagating a fixed-size recurrent state per layer, Mamba naturally supports long-form sequence modeling with constant memory and bandwidth; unlike Transformer/Conformer encoders that maintain a growing key–value cache, it realizes streaming as a pure recurrent update that avoids cache growth and reduces memory traffic on edge devices. These Mamba-based systems are primarily evaluated in non-causal settings and do not explicitly enforce streaming constraints. In this work, we revisit SEMamba [ 4 ] from the perspective of strict streaming deployment and propose a real-time variant, RT-SEMamba. Unlike the original SEMamba, which contains non-causal components and is evaluated offline, RT-SEMamba is made fully causal and operates in an online streaming regime, making it directly applicable to low-latency SE. We further introduce a depth-compression scheme [ 16 , 34 ] in which a deeper 8-layer causal Time-Frequency Mamba (cTF-Mamba) teacher distills its capability into a lightweight 1-layer student. On the VoiceBank-DEMAND (VCTK-DEMAND) dataset [ 42 ] , the distilled RT-SEMamba (8-layer → \\rightarrow 1-layer) consistently improves over a naive 1-layer baseline on common objective and perceptual metrics, while substantially narrowing the gap to the 8-layer teacher. At the same time, it matches the real-time factor of the 1-layer baseline and reduces steady-state RTF by more than 60% compared with the 8-layer teacher under the same algorithmic latency constraint of 25 ms, indicating that RT-SEMamba is suitable for streaming deployment. Figure 1: Architecture of the proposed RT-SEMamba. 2 Related Work 2.1 Mamba and SSM-based sequence models Mamba [ 14 ] is a selective state space sequence model that replaces self-attention with a lightweight linear recurrence. In its simplest form, an SSM maps an input sequence 𝐱 = { x n } n = 1 N \\mathbf{x}=\\{x_{n}\\}_{n=1}^{N} to an output sequence 𝐲 = { y n } n = 1 N \\mathbf{y}=\\{y_{n}\\}_{n=1}^{N} through a latent state 𝐡 \\mathbf{h} : 𝐡 n = 𝐀 ¯ ​ 𝐡 n − 1 + 𝐁 ¯ ​ x n , y n = 𝐂 ​ 𝐡 n , \\mathbf{h}_{n}=\\bar{\\mathbf{A}}\\,\\mathbf{h}_{n-1}+\\bar{\\mathbf{B}}\\,x_{n},\\qquad y_{n}=\\mathbf{C}\\,\\mathbf{h}_{n}, (1) where 𝐀 ¯ \\bar{\\mathbf{A}} and 𝐁 ¯ \\bar{\\mathbf{B}} are discretized state transition matrices and 𝐂 \\mathbf{C} is an output projection. This structure admits both parallel convolutional and recurrent implementations, yielding linear time and memory complexity for long sequences. Building on this backbone, Mamba-style blocks have been applied to time–frequency speech enhancement and separation [ 4 , 25 , 46 , 5 ] . In contrast, RT-SEMamba targets causal streaming speech enhancement with explicit state propagation and progressive distillation from a deeper non-causal teacher. 3 RT-SEMamba Architecture We operate in the complex STFT domain. For a noisy waveform x ⁡ ( t ) x(t) , we compute 𝐗 ⁡ ( f , τ ) = STFT ⁡ ( x ⁡ ( t ) ) = 𝐌 ⁡ ( f , τ ) ​ e j ​ 𝐏 ​ ( f , τ ) , \\mathbf{X}(f,\\tau)=\\mathrm{STFT}(x(t))=\\mathbf{M}(f,\\tau)\\,e^{j\\mathbf{P}(f,\\tau)}, (2) where 𝐌 \\mathbf{M} and 𝐏 \\mathbf{P} denote magnitude and phase. Both teacher and student networks take ( 𝐌 , 𝐏 ) (\\mathbf{M},\\mathbf{P}) as input and predict ( 𝐌 ^ , 𝐏 ^ , 𝐂 ^ ) (\\hat{\\mathbf{M}},\\hat{\\mathbf{P}},\\hat{\\mathbf{C}}) , where 𝐂 ^ = 𝐌 ^ ​ e j ​ 𝐏 ^ \\hat{\\mathbf{C}}=\\hat{\\mathbf{M}}e^{j\\hat{\\mathbf{P}}} is the complex reconstructed spectrum. The enhanced waveform is obtained via inverse STFT from ( 𝐌 ^ , 𝐏 ^ ) (\\hat{\\mathbf{M}},\\hat{\\mathbf{P}}) . 3.1 Causal Formulation and Real-Time Inference RT-SEMamba is implemented in a fully causal manner, with several modifications to the non-causal SEMamba baseline [ 4 ] ; its overall architecture is shown in Fig. 1 . The front-end uses causal STFT/iSTFT at 16 kHz with window size W = 400 W=400 , hop H = 100 H=100 , and center=False for both analysis and synthesis, so the algorithmic latency is bounded by one window (25 ms), and no extra spectral normalization is applied. All temporal convolutions in the encoder and decoders use asymmetric causal padding: for kernel size K K we pad ( K − 1 ) (K-1) zeros on the left and none on the right, with missing past context at the beginning of an utterance filled by zeros. We also replace InstanceNorm2d with channel-wise LayerNorm with causal padding along the time axis, and insert an additional MLP after each cTF-Mamba block to improve per-frame modeling capacity. Time Mamba itself is made uni-directional along time: frames are processed sequentially over t = 1 , … , T t=1,\\dots,T without lookahead, while sequence modeling along the frequency axis within each frame remains bidirectional. In our experiments, we deploy the model in an online streaming configuration, described next. 3.1.1 Streaming inference setup For streaming inference, the model operates online in a 1-frame-in/1-frame-out mode. To avoid recomputing past frames, we propagate a small set of states across time: a temporal frame buffer storing the previous ( K − 1 ) (K-1) feature frames required by causal temporal convolutions in the encoder and decoders, a conv_state buffer that keeps the internal buffer of the depthwise 1D convolution preceding the SSM over the last ( d conv − 1 ) (d_{\\text{conv}}-1) time steps, and an ssm_state that holds the recurrent hidden state h t h_{t} of the selective state-space model. At each time step t t , the model consumes one input frame, updates these states, and emits one enhanced frame, discarding obsolete values. In this setup, the model runs without lookahead, avoids redundant recomputation of past frames, and keeps per-frame compute and memory effectively independent of the sequence length T T . Table 1: Model size, computational complexity per second (MACs (G/s)), and steady-state RTF versus number of cTF-Mamba layers. RTF is measured on a single NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPU, after warm-up. # cTF-Mamba Layers Params (M) MACs (G/s) RTF 1-layer 1.05 20.56 0.11 2-layer 1.29 24.39 0.13 3-layer 1.53 28.21 0.16 4-layer 1.77 32.04 0.19 5-layer 2.01 35.87 0.22 6-layer 2.25 39.70 0.24 7-layer 2.50 43.52 0.27 8-layer 2.74 47.35 0.29 3.2 Knowledge Distillation We use an 8-layer RT-SEMamba as the teacher 𝒯 \\mathcal{T} and a 1-layer RT-SEMamba as the student 𝒮 \\mathcal{S} . For a noisy input ( 𝐌 , 𝐏 ) (\\mathbf{M},\\mathbf{P}) , ( 𝐌 ^ t , 𝐏 ^ t , 𝐂 ^ t ) \\displaystyle(\\hat{\\mathbf{M}}^{t},\\hat{\\mathbf{P}}^{t},\\hat{\\mathbf{C}}^{t}) = 𝒯 ⁡ ( 𝐌 , 𝐏 ) , \\displaystyle=\\mathcal{T}(\\mathbf{M},\\mathbf{P}), (3) ( 𝐌 ^ s , 𝐏 ^ s , 𝐂 ^ s ) \\displaystyle(\\hat{\\mathbf{M}}^{s},\\hat{\\mathbf{P}}^{s},\\hat{\\mathbf{C}}^{s}) = 𝒮 ⁡ ( 𝐌 , 𝐏 ) . \\displaystyle=\\mathcal{S}(\\mathbf{M},\\mathbf{P}). (4) The teacher parameters are frozen. To accelerate convergence, we initialize the student’s encoder, decoder, and single TF-Mamba block with the corresponding weights from the pre-trained teacher. 3.2.1 Output-level distillation We align student predictions with teacher outputs at magnitude, phase, and complex levels: ℒ KD out \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{KD}}^{\\mathrm{out}} = w mag ​ ∥ 𝐌 ^ s − 𝐌 ^ t ∥ 2 2 + w pha ​ ∥ 𝐏 ^ s − 𝐏 ^ t ∥ 2 2 \\displaystyle=w_{\\mathrm{mag}}\\lVert\\hat{\\mathbf{M}}^{s}-\\hat{\\mathbf{M}}^{t}\\rVert_{2}^{2}+w_{\\mathrm{pha}}\\lVert\\hat{\\mathbf{P}}^{s}-\\hat{\\mathbf{P}}^{t}\\rVert_{2}^{2} (5) + w com ​ ∥ 𝐂 ^ s − 𝐂 ^ t ∥ 2 2 . \\displaystyle\\quad+w_{\\mathrm{com}}\\lVert\\hat{\\mathbf{C}}^{s}-\\hat{\\mathbf{C}}^{t}\\rVert_{2}^{2}. (6) We set w mag = 1.0 w_{\\mathrm{mag}}=1.0 , w pha = 0.3 w_{\\mathrm{pha}}=0.3 , w com = 0.5 w_{\\mathrm{com}}=0.5 . 3.2.2 Intermediate feature distillation Let 𝐇 i t \\mathbf{H}_{i}^{t} be the output of the i i -th cTF-Mamba block in the teacher: 𝐇 i t = Φ i t ( 𝐙 i − 1 t ) , i = 1 , … , 8 , \\mathbf{H}_{i}^{t}=\\Phi_{i}^{t}(\\mathbf{Z}_{i-1}^{t}),\\quad i=1,\\dots,8, (7) and let 𝐇 s \\mathbf{H}^{s} denote the student block output. Since the student has only one block, we aggregate teacher features as 𝐇 agg t = 1 8 ​ ∑ i = 1 8 𝐇 i t . \\mathbf{H}_{\\mathrm{agg}}^{t}=\\frac{1}{8}\\sum_{i=1}^{8}\\mathbf{H}_{i}^{t}. (8) To reduce scale mismatch, we normalize features per sample: Norm ⁡ ( 𝐗 ) = 𝐗 − μ ⁡ ( 𝐗 ) σ ⁡ ( 𝐗 ) + ϵ , \\mathrm{Norm}(\\mathbf{X})=\\frac{\\mathbf{X}-\\mu(\\mathbf{X})}{\\sigma(\\mathbf{X})+\\epsilon}, (9) where mean and standard deviation are computed across channel, time, and frequency. The feature distillation loss is ℒ KD feat = ∥ Norm ⁡ ( 𝐇 s ) − Norm ⁡ ( 𝐇 agg t ) ∥ 2 2 . \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{KD}}^{\\mathrm{feat}}=\\lVert\\mathrm{Norm}(\\mathbf{H}^{s})-\\mathrm{Norm}(\\mathbf{H}_{\\mathrm{agg}}^{t})\\rVert_{2}^{2}. (10) 3.2.3 Progressive ramp-up and training objective We gradually introduce the distillation signal using γ ⁡ ( k ) = min ⁡ ( k K ramp , 1 ) , \\gamma(k)=\\min\\left(\\frac{k}{K_{\\mathrm{ramp}}},1\\right), (11) where k k is the training step and K ramp K_{\\mathrm{ramp}} is 10% of the total steps. Let ℒ task \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{task}} denote the original speech enhancement loss, consisting of magnitude, phase, complex, time-domain, and consistency terms, following the same formulation as SEMamba [ 4 ] . The final objective is ℒ total = ℒ task + γ ⁡ ( k ) ​ ( λ out ​ ℒ KD out + λ feat ​ ℒ KD feat ) , \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{total}}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{task}}+\\gamma(k)\\left(\\lambda_{\\mathrm{out}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{KD}}^{\\mathrm{out}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{feat}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{KD}}^{\\mathrm{feat}}\\right), (12) with λ out = 0.5 \\lambda_{\\mathrm{out}}=0.5 and λ feat = 0.1 \\lambda_{\\mathrm{feat}}=0.1 . Table 2: Results on the VCTK-DEMAND dataset for different cTF-Mamba depths and distilled students. Model PESQ CSIG CBAK COVL STOI 1-layer 3.06 4.38 3.61 3.79 0.94 2-layer 3.19 4.51 3.66 3.93 0.95 3-layer 3.20 4.56 3.75 3.96 0.95 4-layer 3.29 4.59 3.7